**New Evidence**
- `Continuity of Policies Supporting Growth and Investments Key to Strengthen Recovery in the Philippines` from the World Bank is useful as baseline context: it says the Philippines was poised to grow 5.7 percent in 2022, and that continuity of reform, inflation control, social assistance, and support for agriculture mattered for recovery. This is more context than direct Marcos execution evidence, but it frames the inherited environment and policy constraints.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Continuity of Policies Supporting Growth and Investments Key to Strengthen Recovery in the Philippines","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-06-08","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/06/08/continuity-of-policies-supporting-growth-and-investments-key-to-strengthen-recovery-in-ph","claim":"The World Bank said the Philippines was poised to grow 5.7 percent in 2022, warned that inflation was a pressing concern, and said policy continuity, targeted social assistance, and support for agriculture would matter for recovery.","locator":"press release lines 24-38","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank country press release with explicit dated macro assessment and policy recommendations.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"In-period baseline and early-2022 context for the Marcos transition.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly inherited and external context; useful as a starting condition rather than proof of Marcos execution.","contrary_evidence":["The piece is a forecast and policy note, not a measurement of Marcos-administered delivery."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.9","8B.10"]}

- Reuters’ cabinet factbox is a strong personnel signal: it identifies Benjamin Diokno as a two-time budget minister heading finance, Arsenio Balisacan as returning planning chief, and Felipe Medalla as the incoming BSP governor. That is direct evidence that Marcos selected an experienced economic team rather than a purely loyalist one.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Factbox-Key figures in Philippine President Marcos' administration","publisher":"Reuters via MarketScreener","publication_date":"2022-06-29","url":"https://www.marketscreener.com/news/latest/Factbox-Key-figures-in-Philippine-President-Marcos-administration-40857917/","claim":"Reuters reported that Benjamin Diokno would lead the Marcos economic team as finance secretary, Arsenio Balisacan would return as planning chief, and Felipe Medalla would replace Diokno at the BSP.","locator":"factbox entries for Diokno, Balisacan, and Medalla","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters is a strong independent wire, though the page is syndicated through MarketScreener.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"In-period; appointments were announced before Marcos was sworn in.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos because these were his cabinet/economic-team selections.","contrary_evidence":["Experienced appointments do not by themselves prove better execution or coordination."],"lenses":["8B.3","8B.4","8B.5"]}

- Marcos’s first executive order was a concrete organizational action, not just rhetoric: it reorganized the Office of the President, said the administration wanted simplicity, economy, and efficiency, and abolished the Presidential Anti-Corruption Commission and the Office of the Cabinet Secretary. That is relevant to his early governing style, though the anti-corruption tradeoff is debatable.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Executive Order No. 1","publisher":"LawPhil / Malacañang","publication_date":"2022-06-30","url":"https://lawphil.net/executive/execord/eo2022/eo_1_2022.html","claim":"Marcos's first EO reorganized the Office of the President, abolished the Presidential Anti-Corruption Commission and the Office of the Cabinet Secretary, and said the administration sought simplicity, economy, and efficiency in the bureaucracy.","locator":"preamble and Sections 1, 7, and 10","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary legal text signed by the president on his first day in office.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"In-period; issued on June 30, 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos as a signed executive action.","contrary_evidence":["Abolishing PACC can be read as streamlining, but also as reducing a specialized anti-corruption channel, so the net effect is ambiguous."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.5","8B.6","8B.8"]}

- The first SONA shows a coherent governing program, not just slogans: Marcos asked Congress to pass 19 priority measures, including rightsizing, budget modernization, e-government, and e-governance, all framed as service-delivery improvements.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PBBM urges Congress to pass admin’s 19 priority measures","publisher":"Presidential Communications Office","publication_date":"2022-07-25","url":"https://pco.gov.ph/news_releases/pbbm-urges-congress-to-pass-admins-19-priority-measures/","claim":"Marcos's first SONA asked Congress to pass 19 priority measures, including National Government Rightsizing, Budget Modernization, E-Government, and E-Governance, to improve government services.","locator":"'Better government services' section","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential communications release summarizing the president's own SONA agenda.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"In-period; first SONA on July 25, 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos because it is his own legislative agenda and public instruction to Congress.","contrary_evidence":["The measures still required congressional passage and later administrative follow-through."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.2","8B.6","8B.9","8B.10"]}

- The SONA transcript sets explicit medium-term targets, which is useful for judging later execution: it named 2022 GDP growth of 5 to 7.5 percent, 2023-2028 growth of 6.5 to 8 percent, poverty at 9 percent by 2028, debt below 60 percent of GDP by 2025, and upper-middle-income status by 2024.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"State of the Nation Address of His Excellency Ferdinand R. Marcos Jr. President of the Philippines to the Congress of the Philippines","publisher":"Presidential Communications Office","publication_date":"2022-07-25","url":"https://pco.gov.ph/presidential-speech/state-of-the-nation-address-of-his-excellency-ferdinand-r-marcos-jr-president-of-the-philippines-to-the-congress-of-the-philippines/","claim":"Marcos set numeric medium-term goals for GDP growth, poverty reduction, debt reduction, and upper-middle-income status, and said the MTFF would anchor annual spending and financing plans.","locator":"MTFF bullet list and the paragraph beginning 'The aforementioned headline goals summarize...'","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct transcript of the president's own address and targets.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"In-period; first SONA in July 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos because these are his announced goals and fiscal architecture.","contrary_evidence":["Targets are commitments, not proof of execution."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.2","8B.7","8B.10"]}

- The FY2023 budget submission is a concrete prioritization signal: it put education, infrastructure, health, agriculture, and social safety nets at the top, with a PHP 5.268 trillion budget and explicit increases for those sectors. This is evidence of translation from agenda to budgeting.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DBM submits 2023 Budget to Congress; Education, infrastructure, health, social protection, agriculture, top budget priorities","publisher":"Department of Budget and Management","publication_date":"2022-08-22","url":"https://www.dbm.gov.ph/index.php/management-2/430-dbm-submits-2023-budget-to-congress-education-infrastructure-health-social-protection-agriculture-top-budget-priorities?highlight=WyJoZmVwIl0%3D","claim":"The Marcos administration submitted a PHP 5.268 trillion FY2023 budget prioritizing education, infrastructure, health, agriculture, and social safety nets, with explicit sectoral increases.","locator":"lines 50-54 and 57-89","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Budget department release with concrete allocations and named priority sectors.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"In-period; submitted in August 2022 for the 2023 budget cycle.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos through his administration's budget proposal and priority-setting.","contrary_evidence":["The budget is a plan and appropriation proposal; delivery still depends on execution by agencies and Congress."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.6","8B.7","8B.10"]}

- Marcos repeated the program directly to investors in September 2022, describing the eight-point agenda as a near-term response to inflation and food security plus a medium-term strategy for jobs, infrastructure, human capital, digitalization, innovation, a green and blue economy, and livable communities. That is a clear revealed program.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Road to progress: PBBM touts eight-point socio-economic agenda before US investors, biz leaders","publisher":"Presidential Communications Office","publication_date":"2022-09-23","url":"https://pco.gov.ph/news_releases/road-to-progress-pbbm-touts-eight-point-socio-economic-agenda-before-us-investors-biz-leaders/","claim":"Marcos presented the eight-point socioeconomic agenda to investors, saying it would address inflation and food security in the near term and jobs, infrastructure, human capital, digitalization, innovation, and livable communities over the medium term.","locator":"opening paragraphs and quoted summary of the agenda","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official release summarizing the president's own public presentation to investors.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"In-period; September 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos because it is his own public articulation of the governing program.","contrary_evidence":["Investor-facing rhetoric can overstate readiness and does not itself prove implementation."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.2","8B.6","8B.10"]}

- Marcos also made a concrete early personnel move on October 4, 2022, when he led the oath-taking of cabinet members including Diokno, Balisacan, Pascual, Tulfo, Bautista, Solidum, Lotilla, Ople, and Acuzar. This matters because it shows he actually staffed the program, not just announced it.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PBBM leads oath-taking of Cabinet members in Malacañang","publisher":"Presidential Communications Office","publication_date":"2022-10-04","url":"https://pco.gov.ph/news_releases/pbbm-leads-oath-taking-of-cabinet-members-in-malacanang/","claim":"Marcos led the oath-taking of reappointed and ad interim cabinet members including Benjamin Diokno, Arsenio Balisacan, Alfredo Pascual, Renato Solidum Jr., Raphael Lotilla, and others.","locator":"bullet list of cabinet appointees","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official presidential release listing the actual appointees sworn into office.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"In-period; October 4, 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos as the appointing authority.","contrary_evidence":["Cabinet swearing-in does not itself prove effective coordination or delivery."],"lenses":["8B.3","8B.4","8B.5","8B.9"]}

- The agrarian-reform moratorium is narrower than the whole economic portfolio, but it is a real implementation step tied to food security and farm investment rather than just rhetoric.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Marcos Jr.: Agrarian reform program must continue","publisher":"Presidential Communications Office","publication_date":"2022-10-05","url":"https://pco.gov.ph/news_releases/marcos-jr-agrarian-reform-program-must-continue/","claim":"Marcos said he would issue an EO imposing a one-year moratorium on land amortization and interest for agrarian reform beneficiaries and said this would help farmers invest in their farms and support food security.","locator":"article body quoting the first SONA commitment and EO 4 rationale","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official release linking a promised policy to a later presidential order and explaining its purpose.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"In-period; October 2022 follow-through on a July 2022 commitment.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos as a signed executive action.","contrary_evidence":["This is a sector-specific measure; it does not by itself show broad economic competence.","The effects would take time and depend on administrative rollout."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.6","8B.9","8B.10"]}

- The investment-policy streamlining piece shows further operationalization: DOF said the Marcos administration had amended investment laws and BOT rules to widen space for foreign firms and make PPP participation more attractive. That is a concrete policy path, though some reforms predated Marcos and the attribution is therefore partial.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Marcos admin streamlines private sector participation processes to encourage investments","publisher":"Department of Finance","publication_date":"2022-10-24","url":"https://www.dof.gov.ph/marcos-admin-streamlines-private-sector-participation-processes-to-encourage-investments/","claim":"The Marcos administration said it had streamlined private-sector participation through amended investment laws and revised BOT rules to attract more domestic and foreign investment and make PPPs more attractive.","locator":"first three paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official finance-ministry release describing enacted or operationalized investment reforms and their intended purpose.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"In-period; October 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Partially attributable to Marcos; some reforms were inherited or legislated earlier, but the administration is claiming operational ownership and sequencing.","contrary_evidence":["Some of the enabling laws were passed before Marcos took office, so not all credit belongs to him.","Claims about improved investor response are forward-looking rather than outcome proof."],"lenses":["8B.2","8B.3","8B.6","8B.8"]}

- The IMF’s 2022 Article IV is strong independent corroboration that the new administration had a defined blueprint and faced the right macro-management tasks: price stability, fiscal buffers, and long-term growth. It is not evidence of success by itself, but it helps judge whether the administration had a coherent program.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Philippines: 2022 Article IV Consultation-Press Release; and Staff Report","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-12-15","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/publications/cr/issues/2022/12/15/philippines-2022-article-iv-consultation-press-release-and-staff-report-526983","claim":"The IMF said the new administration had a blueprint for an inclusive, sustainable, and green recovery, but still faced three challenges: choosing the right policy mix for recovery and price stability, building fiscal buffers, and raising long-term growth.","locator":"Summary section lines 272-273","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent IMF country report with a clear summary of the administration's stated blueprint and macro tasks.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"In-period; December 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly tied to Marcos's administration because the report describes its own blueprint and policy challenge set.","contrary_evidence":["The IMF also implies the blueprint was still at the challenge-setting stage rather than proven execution."],"lenses":["8B.1","8B.2","8B.7","8B.9","8B.10"]}

- The World Bank’s June 2022 update is useful for separating inherited conditions from Marcos-specific execution: it forecast recovery, stressed continuity of growth-and-investment policies, and warned that inflation would dent consumption and poverty outcomes. This helps avoid crediting or blaming Marcos for pre-existing recovery dynamics.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Continuity of Policies Supporting Growth and Investments Key to Strengthen Recovery in the Philippines","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-06-08","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/06/08/continuity-of-policies-supporting-growth-and-investments-key-to-strengthen-recovery-in-ph","claim":"The World Bank said the Philippines was poised to grow 5.7 percent in 2022 and that inflation, global shocks, and the need for continuity in pro-growth and investment policies shaped the recovery outlook.","locator":"press release lines 24-38","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent country press release with specific macro forecast and policy context.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"In-period baseline and early-2022 macro context.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly inherited and external context; not a direct measure of Marcos execution.","contrary_evidence":["The piece does not evaluate Marcos's own implementation, only the environment he inherited."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.9","8B.10"]}

- PSA’s 2022 StatDev release is one of the best outcome summaries for the period: GDP grew 7.6 percent, unemployment fell to 5.4 percent, and youth unemployment also improved, but only four of 15 sectoral chapters had average performance and some targets remained unmet. That is mixed evidence, not a clean success story.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PSA releases the 2022 Statistical Indicators on Philippine Development","publisher":"Philippine Statistics Authority","publication_date":"2023-07-17","url":"https://psa.gov.ph/content/psa-releases-2022-statistical-indicators-philippine-development","claim":"The PSA said GDP grew 7.6 percent in 2022 and unemployment fell to 5.4 percent, but only four of 15 sectoral chapters had average performance and several development targets were still unmet.","locator":"lines 346-357 and 371-440","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official statistical release with the annual 2022 scorecard and target comparisons.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective 2023 release reporting 2022 outcomes.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Marcos-era execution, but also to post-pandemic rebound and inherited trend effects.","contrary_evidence":["The same release notes mixed performance across sectors and unmet targets, so it does not prove broad execution success."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.8","8B.10"]}

- The BSP inflation table shows the administration’s main macro headwind in 2022: annual average inflation was 5.8 percent, with December inflation at 8.1 percent. This is a strong negative outcome signal, though not all of it is president-attributable.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Inflation Rates","publisher":"Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas","publication_date":"unknown","url":"https://www.bsp.gov.ph/statistics/Prices/tab34_inf_2018_data.aspx","claim":"The BSP table shows annual average inflation of 5.8 percent in 2022 and December 2022 inflation of 8.1 percent.","locator":"Table 34, rows for annual average and December 2022","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official BSP statistical table with clear annual and monthly inflation data.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"In-period macro outcome for 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to the Marcos administration's macro management, but heavily affected by global and inherited price shocks.","contrary_evidence":["Inflation is not solely caused by the ruler; global food, fuel, and supply shocks were major contributors."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.9","8B.10"]}

- The BSP’s November 2022 policy report shows the central bank tightening hard in response, raising the RRP to 5.0 percent and revising inflation forecasts upward. That does not prove Marcos competence or failure by itself, but it corroborates that the administration was operating under real inflation stress and that policy adaptation was necessary.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Monetary Policy Report November 2022","publisher":"Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas","publication_date":"2022-11-17","url":"https://www.bsp.gov.ph/SitePages/PriceStability/FullReportMPR/MonetaryPolicyReport_Full_November2022.aspx/%7B%7BGetWebAbsoluteURL%28%29%20%2B%20%27/Price%20Stability/MonetaryPolicyReport/MonetaryPolicySummary_November2022.pdf%27%7D%7D","claim":"At its November 17, 2022 meeting, the BSP raised the overnight reverse repurchase rate to 5.0 percent and revised inflation forecasts to 5.8 percent for 2022 and 4.3 percent for 2023.","locator":"Monetary Policy Summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official central-bank policy summary with explicit rate decision and forecast revisions.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"In-period; November 2022 macro response.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly an independent BSP response, not direct presidential action, but it reflects the macro conditions the administration faced.","contrary_evidence":["The BSP action is not a direct Marcos decision, so it can only be used as macro context and policy-environment corroboration."],"lenses":["8B.8","8B.9","8B.10"]}

- The World Bank’s December 2022 remarks show a mixed trajectory: strong domestic demand and a 7.6 percent Q3 growth rate, but also high inflation, rising rates, slower global growth, learning losses, and worsening child malnutrition. That is useful for judging whether growth was translating into resilient progress rather than just a rebound.  
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Opening remarks at the launch of the Philippines Economic Update December 2022 Edition","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12-12","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/speech/2022/12/12/opening-remarks-at-the-launch-of-the-philippines-economic-update-dec-2022-ed","claim":"The World Bank said strong domestic demand was driving growth, but high inflation and rising rates would slow the economy and could worsen child malnutrition and learning losses unless policy focused on health, education, and agriculture.","locator":"opening remarks lines 24-38 and the later policy-focus paragraph","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent World Bank remarks summarizing the December 2022 outlook and risks.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"In-period; December 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Marcos-era policy choices, but also heavily shaped by post-pandemic rebound and global slowdown.","contrary_evidence":["This is still an outlook note, not a direct measure of implementation success.","Some growth and demand strength were inherited recovery effects rather than direct new-policy output."],"lenses":["8B.7","8B.9","8B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
- I screened roughly 30 documents across official presidential releases, budget and finance pages, BSP, PSA, LawPhil, IMF, World Bank, Reuters/MarketScreener, PIA, PNA, BusinessWorld, GMA, and a few later follow-ups.
- I kept the best non-duplicative evidence on program articulation, budget translation, appointments, implementation steps, macro outcomes, and macro constraints.
- I rejected or downgraded several items because they duplicated the same SONA/budget story without adding a new claim, were broader 2023-2024 follow-ups with no extra 2022 execution signal, or were too general and not attributable to Marcos specifically.
- I also excluded several pre-office or inherited baseline items unless they directly helped explain the starting conditions the 2022 administration inherited.
- I did not treat later integrity or rights material as competence evidence unless it directly illuminated program execution, staffing, or administrative follow-through.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Direct evidence of agency-level implementation in 2022 is still thinner than evidence of agenda-setting and budgeting.
- The strongest open question is whether the cabinet and economic team actually coordinated better across departments, beyond being experienced.
- More material would help on whether the FY2023 budget, investment reforms, and food-security measures produced measurable 2022-end or early-2023 changes in investment, prices, and household welfare.
- Regional and sectoral unevenness is only partially visible here; better subnational evidence would sharpen the execution picture.
- More search would likely help, especially on audits, rollout reports, and 2023 implementation documents that explicitly trace 2022 decisions to later outcomes.
